<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puerto Alonso</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Porto Acre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Puerto Alonso was a Bolivian customs post on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> River, set up in 1899 to tax <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> exported by Brazilian tappers. Its opening provoked Luis Gálvez to proclaim the Independent State of Acre in July 1899. The territory passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-petropolis">Treaty of Petrópolis</a> in 1903, and the town is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> Acre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puerto-alonso.jpg" alt="Puerto Alonso" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agência de Notícias do Acre · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casa_em_Porto_Acre_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
